    \u0441\u0431\u043e\u0440\u043d\u0438\u043a \u0441\u0442\u0438\u0445\u043e\u0432 \u0412\u0435\u0440\u0433\u0438\u043b\u0438\u044f,
    \u0440\u0435\u043b\u0438\u0433\u0438\u043e\u0437\u043d\u044b\u0439 \u0442\u0440\u0430\u043a\u0442\u0430\u0442
    \u00ab\u041f\u043e\u0434\u0440\u0430\u0436\u0430\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0425\u0440\u0438\u0441\u0442\u0443\u00bb,
    \u00ab\u041d\u0435\u0431\u0435\u0441\u043d\u0443\u044e \u043c\u0435\u0445\u0430\u043d\u0438\u043a\u0443\u00bb
    \u041b\u0430\u043f\u043b\u0430\u0441\u0430 \u0438 \u00ab\u0422\u0440\u0430\u043a\u0442\u0430\u0442
    \u043e\u0431 \u0430\u043d\u0430\u043b\u0438\u0442\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0445
    \u0444\u0443\u043d\u043a\u0446\u0438\u044f\u0445\u00bb \u041b\u0430\u0433\u0440\u0430\u043d\u0436\u0430."
  interestingFactsEn: "Kosha's life is full of curious episodes that paint it not just as a great scientist, but as a very unusual person, these are the most interesting and interesting facts from his biography, gathered together.\n\n▪ Prophecy and the way of life: When Koshi was still a child, Joseph-Louis Lagrange, a close friend of the family, predicted his genius. He even warned Kosha's father not to show the boy math books until he was 17 years old to get a good human education.He first chose a career as a military engineer and worked to strengthen the port in Cherbury during Napoleon.\n\n♪ The fertility and \"Kosha\" journey - Balzac in mathematics: Koshi was incredibly productive. The volume of his writing compares him to Honore de Balzac, who wrote 90 volumes of \"The Human Comedy.\" Kosha left over 800 scientific works and five textbooks. His full collection of essays is 27 volumes. The King's way in mathematics: Kosha became one of the few mathematicians whose life path crossed with the royals.He was the tutor of the grandson of the overthrown King Charles X in Prague, for which the expelled king gave him the title of baron. \"The next memoir...\": His last words at the Academy of Sciences were: \"I will explain this in more detail in the next memoir.\"\n\nIn 1830, after the July Revolution, he chose to lose all his posts and go to voluntary exile, in order not to swear allegiance to the new King Louis Philipp.However, when Ostrobray was in Paris, Koshi approved and published his mathematical work and then bought a scientist from prison, and he was a hard teacher: students and colleagues considered him to be difficult to communicate with, and he was known for his intransigence in matters of religion and politics, which made him many enemies in the scientific community.\n\nCouriez and surprises Engineering jokes: During the work of engineer Kosha, he was working on repairs to Parisian analysis, and his son, the young Duke of Bordeaux, reminded him of this with childish anger, saying that Koshi began his career in Paris pipes. World recognition: Koshi's name is inscribed on the Eiffel Tower on the list of the 72 greatest scientists in France.Just four books: A young engineer, Kosha, took four books to work for the first time in Cherbour: a collection of poems from Vergilia, a religious tract called \"Imitation of Christ\", \"Sky Mechanic\" Laplas, and \"Tactate on Analytical Functions\" Lagranga."
